  task edits are written as mutable blobs now, notifications later have to reconstruct changes by diffing, which is the
  usual reason notification systems end up vague and untrustworthy ("something changed"). Recording an event at mutation
  time is nearly free today and awkward to retrofit.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much of that chain you want to commit to now, and whether the deliverable is running code or a target
  design.

  - [1mBuild the chain in order[0m — spec #1 is tasks + storage, with the change-event log designed in from the start. You get
    a working app soonest, and notifications drop in cleanly later. Costs: notifications aren't in your hands for a
    while. Nothing here is expensive to change later except the event log, which is why we'd do it now.
  - [1mDesign notifications fully now, build backwards[0m — a spec for the end state, no code yet. Buys a north star and
    catches constraints early. Costs: it's a design against an app that doesn't exist, so parts of it will be
    speculative and get revised once tasks are real.
  - [1mScope down hard[0m — single user, one browser, local storage, an in-app "changed since you last looked" list. No
    accounts, no server, no email. Buys something real and demoable quickly. Costs: it rules out the collaborative
    meaning of "users," and a real multi-user system later would replace most of it rather than extend it.

  My recommendation is the first. The request's value is in items 4–5, but its risk is entirely in item 3, and building
  the chain in order is the only option that gets the risky part right while still producing a working app. I'd fold the
  event log into spec #1 explicitly as groundwork for notifications, so it isn't dropped as YAGNI.
